<p>Read this page before beginning the dialogues. The historical context will help you follow what is at stake in each reading.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="tabs"><div class="tabs__bar" role="tablist"><button class="tabs__tab tabs__tab--active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-0" id="tabs-0-tab-0">▸ 1 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Athens and the world Socrates lived in</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-1" id="tabs-0-tab-1">▸ 2 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Socrates</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-2" id="tabs-0-tab-2">▸ 3 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Plato</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-3" id="tabs-0-tab-3">▸ 4 &nbsp;—&nbsp; The three dialogues: a roadmap</button></div><div class="tabs__panel tabs__panel--active" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-0" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-0"><div>
<p>Socrates lived in Athens from 469 to 399 BCE, during one of the most turbulent and intellectually extraordinary periods in Western history. Athens had recently led the Greek city-states to victory over the Persian Empire, and in the aftermath it became the dominant political and cultural force in the Greek world. The Parthenon was built during Socrates' lifetime. So was Athenian democracy — and so, eventually, was its collapse.</p>
<p>By the time Socrates was put on trial in 399 BCE, Athens had just lost a disastrous war with Sparta, suffered a brutal oligarchic coup, and was in the process of rebuilding a democracy that many citizens blamed for the city's decline. Into this politically charged atmosphere stepped a man whose entire life’s work consisted of publicly questioning the beliefs of Athens’ most prominent citizens and finding them wanting.</p>
<p>The charges against him — impiety and corrupting the youth — make more sense in this context. Socrates was not simply an irritating philosopher. He was, to many Athenians, a destabilizing force at a moment when the city could least afford destabilization.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Keep in mind while reading</p>
<p>The three dialogues in this unit — the <em>Euthyphro</em>, the <em>Apology</em>, and the <em>Crito</em> — are set in the days immediately before and after Socrates’ trial and execution. They are not abstract philosophical exercises. They are a man’s response to imminent death.</p>
</div></div>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-1" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-1" hidden=""><div>
<p>Socrates was born in 469 BCE to a stonemason and a midwife. He grew up in Athens, received a standard Athenian education in poetry, music, and athletics, and served in the military. By mid-life he had become a fixture of the Athenian agora — the public marketplace — where he spent his days in conversation with anyone willing to engage him. He charged no fees, founded no school, and wrote nothing.</p>
<p>Everything we know about Socrates comes from others — primarily Plato, but also Xenophon and the comic playwright Aristophanes, who mocked him in the <em>Clouds</em>. The challenge for readers of Plato’s dialogues is that we cannot always be certain where Socrates ends and Plato begins.</p>
<h3>The Oracle and the mission</h3>
<p>Socrates claimed that his philosophical mission began with a pronouncement from the Oracle at Delphi, who reportedly declared that no one in Athens was wiser than Socrates. Astounded, Socrates set out to disprove the Oracle by finding someone wiser than himself — a politician, a poet, a craftsman. Each time, he discovered that the person’s reputation for wisdom far exceeded their actual understanding. They believed they knew things they did not know.</p>
<p>Socrates concluded that the Oracle was right, but not because he possessed special knowledge. He was wisest because he alone knew that he did not know. This — intellectual humility in the face of hard questions — is what he called <strong>Socratic ignorance</strong>, and it became the engine of his entire method.</p>
<h3>The examined life</h3>
<p>In the <em>Apology</em>, Socrates makes one of the most famous claims in the history of philosophy: that <em>the unexamined life is not worth living</em>. This is not an invitation to navel-gazing. It is a claim about what it means to be a rational agent. To live without subjecting your beliefs to examination — without asking whether what you take for granted is actually true — is to sleepwalk through existence.</p>
<p>This commitment to examination is not without cost. Socrates was convicted of impiety and corrupting the youth of Athens and sentenced to death. He was 70 years old. He refused to escape when given the chance, refused to propose a lesser penalty that might have satisfied his accusers, and drank the hemlock himself. His death was, in its own way, the final act of the examined life.</p>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
