</div></div>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-2" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-2" hidden=""><div>
<p>Plato was born around 428 BCE into one of Athens’ most prominent aristocratic families. He became a student of Socrates as a young man, and Socrates’ trial and execution in 399 BCE shaped the rest of his intellectual life. The question of how a just man could be condemned by a democratic city — and what that implies about democracy, justice, and the relationship between the individual and the state — runs through nearly everything Plato wrote.</p>
<p>Plato went on to found the Academy in Athens around 387 BCE, one of the earliest institutions of higher learning in the Western world. He wrote extensively, and unlike Socrates, his work survives. He died around 348 BCE.</p>
<h3>The dialogues and their problem</h3>
<p>Plato wrote in the form of philosophical dialogues — scripted conversations, usually with Socrates as the main character. The early dialogues, which include the <em>Euthyphro</em>, <em>Apology</em>, and <em>Crito</em>, are generally considered the most historically reliable portraits of Socrates. The later dialogues, including the <em>Republic</em>, present increasingly systematic philosophical positions that are generally attributed to Plato himself rather than Socrates.</p>
<p>This creates a persistent interpretive challenge: in any given dialogue, you are reading Plato’s dramatization of Socrates’ thought. You cannot assume the historical Socrates held exactly the positions his character expresses. What you can assume is that Plato found them worth putting into Socrates’ mouth — which is itself philosophically significant.</p>
<h3>The Theory of Forms</h3>
<p>Plato’s most distinctive philosophical contribution is the <strong>Theory of Forms</strong> — the idea that the physical world we perceive through our senses is not the most real world. Behind or above it lies a realm of eternal, unchanging, perfect <em>Forms</em> (sometimes translated as <em>Ideas</em>): the Form of Justice, the Form of Beauty, the Form of the Good. The things we see and experience are imperfect copies or instances of these Forms.</p>
<p>This theory has deep roots in the Socratic dialogues. When Socrates asks Euthyphro “what is piety?” he is not asking for examples of pious acts. He is asking for the <em>Form</em> of piety — the single thing that all pious acts have in common, the standard by which we recognize piety when we see it. The search for definitions in the early dialogues is, in embryo, the search for Forms.</p>
<div>
<p>Looking ahead</p>
<p>The Theory of Forms, Plato’s epistemology, and his political philosophy are developed further in the <em>Republic</em>, which appears later in this unit. The early dialogues lay the groundwork; the <em>Republic</em> builds the structure.</p>
</div>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-3" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-3" hidden=""><div>
<p>The three dialogues in this unit are set across a span of a few days. Read in sequence, they tell a single story — not just about Socrates’ death, but about the relationship between philosophy, justice, and civic obligation.</p>
<h3>The Euthyphro</h3>
<p>Set on the steps of the court where Socrates is about to be arraigned. Socrates meets Euthyphro, a religious expert who is prosecuting his own father for impiety. The conversation turns to the question: <em>what is piety?</em> Euthyphro offers several definitions; Socrates dismantles each one. The dialogue ends without a resolution — but it contains one of the most important arguments in the history of philosophy of religion.</p>
<h3>The Apology</h3>
<p>Socrates’ speech at his own trial. He defends his life’s work, explains the Oracle story, and argues that the unexamined life is not worth living. He is convicted and sentenced to death. The <em>Apology</em> is one of the clearest articulations in all of philosophy of what it means to live by one’s convictions regardless of consequence.</p>
<h3>The Crito</h3>
<p>Set in Socrates’ prison cell. His friend Crito arrives before dawn with an escape plan. Socrates refuses to go. The dialogue asks: do we have an obligation to obey laws we believe are unjust? Socrates’ answer — carefully argued, not merely asserted — is one of the foundational texts of political philosophy.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A note on the reading</p>
<p>These dialogues are short by philosophical standards — but they are not easy. Socrates’ arguments are compressed, his moves are sometimes subtle, and the dramatic framing can distract from the logical structure. When you encounter a step in an argument that doesn’t immediately make sense, slow down and ask: what is the premise here, and what is he trying to conclude? The <strong>Logic Reference</strong> page in Unit 1 can help if you need to revisit argument structure before you begin.</p>
</div></div>
